			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1523139">
									

<p>but if she wasn't innocent, and she'd know that no such person existed, then why would she have hope?</p>
<p>There's a story&#8230;.</p>
<p>Closing arguments at a murder trial. The defense attorney is a master orator, and is hammering on the fact that the victim's body was never found. &quot;And perhaps&quot;, he says in that wonderful voice of his, &quot;perhaps the alleged 'victim' has been found alive an hour ago, and is about to walk through that courtroom door!&quot;</p>
<p>and so persuasive and evocative is he, that all the jurors turn around and look at the door.</p>
<p>No one walks through the door.</p>
<p>&quot;You see,&quot; the defense attorney says, &quot;you all have a reasonable doubt as to whether my client killed anyone.&quot;</p>
<p>Then it's the prosecutor's turn.</p>
<p>&quot;You all turned to look at the door&quot;, he says, &quot;because you all thought it might be possible that the victim might walk through it. But the defendant did not, because he <em>knows</em> that the victim is dead.&quot;</p>

